					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dr-recoil" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6923102); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6923102&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607780391" alt="Dr_Recoil" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6923102)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dr-recoil" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6923102); return false;" >Dr_Recoil</a></span> <span class="odate time_1607779261 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">12 Dec 2020 13:21</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13909299/scp-idea:the-necro-matriarch-broadcast-manifestations">SCP Idea: The Necro-Matriarch Broadcast Manifestations</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4843041">
									

<p>Okay, I understood your points. I also felt like most of it is caused to my inability to explain my concept entirely, due to the word count constraint and restriction of sharing the sandbox page for it in this forum; with the latter only being allowed when requested by the reviewer. Nevertheless, I am now gonna explain all of it.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;text&nbsp;</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;hide&nbsp;text</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Makes me rather skeptical, since there are plenty of cases where grandmothers have been distant or psychotic or outright abusive—do those grandmothers still give people their favorite food and good chats? What happens if someone loathes their grandmother and just summons her to scream at her? Same goes for backgrounds involving barriers such as, say, language barriers or the case of a grandmother passing away before she ever got to meet her grandchildren. What happens in those cases?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>- Ah yes, you see, I've already thought some of that beforehand while writing the draft for my concept at the sandbox, specifically in its test/interaction logs. For the ones who have a bad memory, no matter how severe it is, that same &quot;grandma&quot; will be completely different. She will still try to ask for forgiveness and make amends to their grandchild in a way, unless of course if the grandchild is still<br />
really mad at them; a topic I manage to edit in at the draft as I write this, thanks for the clarification!<br />
<span style="white-space: pre-wrap;">&#32;</span><br />
- As for the language barrier problem, It will be no worry since the anomaly would be able to speak their language at ease, due to you know, them having an innate knowledge of the subject's past and current life.<br />
<span style="white-space: pre-wrap;">&#32;</span><br />
- Lastly, for the ones who died right away. If no other grandma is really available, even the rude ones who manage to live longer, the radio will just remain silent. These last two are probably the only ones I’ve managed to think about beforehand.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Do you intend to have any background context as to why this anomaly exists the way it does?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I am sorry, I didn’t quite catch that. Could you please elaborate it?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Don't have the Foundation just conveniently find out about the anomaly from a random convenient online post. Maybe have them start investigating when they start fielding reports from people complaining about being angry at/sad about Dead Grandma randomly showing up when they're just trying to listen to the radio.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>- Oh yeah, about that. I am planning to make “convenient online post” to be like the debut of the anomaly in the Foundation’s eyes, meaning the proceeding cases will only happen once the Foundation finds the very first instance. Almost as if the mysterious creature of this anomaly is trying to play games with the Foundation, and benefit them at the same time.<br />
<span style="white-space: pre-wrap;">&#32;</span><br />
- Also, it kinda makes sense for them to monitor post in the Parawatch website, since their posts might actually refer to potential anomalies, you know, mandatory verifications. About the radio interruption though, do antique radios still function normally like they do, because I thought they were just for display.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Don't have the Foundation site's morale improve excessively—even when the masquerade has been broken, the Foundation shouldn't be encouraging its employees to start relying on anomalies, especially ones they have no control over (for people who develop dangerous dependencies on being able to talk to Dead Grandma, what happens if the broadcasts suddenly stop? Best to not let people become that dependent in the first place)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>- Copy that, I only written the increased percentage in the draft, in about 35.5 percent. If that is considered too much, I’ll just decrease it in a much considerable lower amount, appropriate for their profession.<br />
- That’s also the reason why I planned the interactions to be scheduled and regulated, like I’ve written in the article.<br />
<span style="white-space: pre-wrap;">&#32;</span><br />
- The last thing you said is also great for a possible Tale about this skip, so thanks again.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Have the Foundation treat the anomaly with the usual suspicion and skepticism, rather than just welcoming it nigh-immediately and letting people schedule interactions. You can hint at restrictions based on psychological reports and family history being in place in the containment procedures.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Alrighty then.</p>
</div>
