			<div class="content" id="post-content-4842973">
									

<p>Hello hello! Not a critter here, but I can still read and give opinions.<br />
So, the idea as presented is a good starting point, but I feel as though that's really what it is. A start.</p>
<p>As JamaisJ above (at least, I think their post will be above mine) noted, you lack a narrative. A few ideas on what that may be, for example, could be an investigation into who made the dice. The devil? Why? Why would the devil make dice that have seemingly random and ironic effects, instead of things that might be more 'in-character' for what people think about the devil?</p>
<p>Or maybe it was not our boy Lucifer at all; maybe it was some other otherwordly force that has some other, different association with either the number 6 or the number 9 (or just the number 3 in general, with 18 being the highest possible multiple obtainable on three dice)? If so, what sort of entity is it? Do people know it? Why make dice? How did it lose them? You can let your imagination run wild there, if you go that route.</p>
<p>Or maybe yet it was some person who was trying to honor/appease/give something to an entity, devil or not, for some reason, and ended up with this dice. This is also a route you could go for in terms of narrative, I feel.</p>
<p>Of course, you could do something completely different too; I just wanted to give the first prompt that came to mind, and hopefully it might give you some ideas too.</p>
<p>The last point I want to bring up is how the dice disappear at the end, which is a recorded effect, implying then- if I understand correctly- that the item is now uncontained and what they have is a pretty non-anomalous (appearing from thin air aside) note? Not a critique or anything, just wanna make sure I got this right.</p>

							</div>

								
					
