																	<a href="/forum/t-13909299/scp-idea:the-necro-matriarch-broadcast-manifestations#post-4842811">Re: SCP Idea: The Necro-Matriarch Broadcast Manifestations</a>
									</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1404533&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607780391" alt="Zyn" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1404533)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;" >Zyn</a></span> <span class="odate time_1607760771 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">12 Dec 2020 08:12</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13909299/scp-idea:the-necro-matriarch-broadcast-manifestations">SCP Idea: The Necro-Matriarch Broadcast Manifestations</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4842811">
									

<p>Got a PM about this. As a preface, I'm not familiar with Parawatch, so I'll be reviewing this from the perspective of someone for whom the addition of Parawatch doesn't contribute anything immediately positive to the concept. With regards to credit someone as co-author, you would note their name in an author page/on your sandbox draft, and when the piece is mainlisted you would ask a staff member to update the Attribution page to associate your names with the article.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I'll admit that the initial premise of this feels reminiscent of my own <a href="/scp-348">SCP-348</a>, even down to the &quot;it's not certain whether it's the actual presence of the family member being manifested&quot; angle. That said, my main issue about this is that it's kind of <em>too</em> perfect and nice, and also seems to assume that people will always have heartfelt happy reactions to their grandmother being manifested. This portion:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They instantaneously manifest your favorite food and gives you good chat about your life, while a suiting instrumental classical/swing/jazz music accompanies the scenery.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>makes me rather skeptical, since there are plenty of cases where grandmothers have been distant or psychotic or outright abusive&#8212;do those grandmothers still give people their favorite food and good chats? What happens if someone loathes their grandmother and just summons her to scream at her? Same goes for backgrounds involving barriers such as, say, language barriers or the case of a grandmother passing away before she ever got to meet her grandchildren. What happens in those cases?</p>
<p>The narrative is also fairly plain and kind of unimpressive&#8212;it boils down to &quot;Foundation investigates, finds thing, brings thing to others, lots of people are suddenly and conveniently happy&quot;. The investigation background feels almost lazy for me as a reader; do you intend to have any background context as to why this anomaly exists the way it does?</p>
<p>Also, ending note:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Nevertheless, the effects of this anomaly is not memetic, making the heartfelt reactions genuine.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not sure why anyone would expect the anomaly to be memetic, since there's no inherent transfer of anomalous information involved. I think you may have been thinking of a mind-affecting anomaly? For more information on memetics, see the <a href="/understanding-memetics">Understanding Memetics</a> page.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Overall, I feel like for this to work, you're going to need more than just the Broken Masquerade setup to make this &quot;found nice thing, everyone's happy&quot; kinda flat story feel more meaningful and memorable. In fact, I myself would be more impressed by the story if the Foundation was dealing with lots of cases where the good intentions of the creator went <em>wrong</em> and pulled a victory via emphasizing the importance of counseling and mental wellness in familial relations or the like. At any rate, have the nice things happen in a way that they need to be earned/maintained, rather than just handed out with few/no problems at all.</p>
<p>Other suggestions:</p>
<ul>
<li>don't have the Foundation just conveniently find out about the anomaly from a random convenient online post. Maybe have them start investigating when they start fielding reports from people complaining about being angry at/sad about Dead Grandma randomly showing up when they're just trying to listen to the radio.</li>
<li>don't have the Foundation site's morale improve excessively&#8212;even when the masquerade has been broken, the Foundation shouldn't be encouraging its employees to start relying on anomalies, especially ones they have no control over (for people who develop dangerous dependencies on being able to talk to Dead Grandma, what happens if the broadcasts suddenly stop? Best to not let people become that dependent in the first place)</li>
<li>have the Foundation treat the anomaly with the usual suspicion and skepticism, rather than just welcoming it nigh-immediately and letting people schedule interactions. You can hint at restrictions based on psychological reports and family history being in place in the containment procedures.</li>
</ul>

							</div>

								
					
